# Remote Development Environment (RDE)

RDEs are Ubuntu servers running in AWS, used as personal cloud dev environments.

## Naming Convention

Each RDE is named after the developer's GitHub username:

```
<github-username>-rde.fbx.im
```

## Access Methods

**Via the in-house `rde` CLI (preferred):**
```bash
rde ssh
```

**Via raw SSH:**
```bash
ssh -i ~/.ssh/Dev.pem ubuntu@<github-username>-rde.fbx.im
```

## Important

When asked to make code changes, always edit files **locally** in the workspace. Never SSH into the RDE to edit files directly — `rde sync` will propagate local changes to the RDE.

## File Sync

The `rde` CLI can sync local files to the RDE:

```bash
# Continuous sync (activate + monitor together)
rde sync activate <repo-name>
rde sync monitor <repo-name>

# One-time sync
rde sync now <repo-name>
```

## Services

Fundbox services are managed via `supervisorctl`:

```bash
sudo supervisorctl status
sudo supervisorctl restart <service_name>
```

## Paths

| Purpose | Path |
|---|---|
| Fundbox repos | `/opt/fundbox/<repo_name>/` |
| Service logs | `/opt/fundbox/logs/<service_name>.log` |
| Virtual environments | `/opt/fundbox/venvs/<service_name>/` |

Venv installation is handled by two scripts in `deployment/rde/scripts/`:
- `change_tracker.py` — orchestrates venv creation and updates
- `pip_installer.sh` — handles the actual pip package installation

## Notes

- The SSH key used is `~/.ssh/Dev.pem`
- The default user is `ubuntu`
- RDEs live in the AWS cloud and are shared/personal environments for development and investigation
